window.docContents[191].resolve({"/Module-topology___-noetherian-rings-and-the-structure-sheaf/#Adic-Spaces-___-Lean-blueprint--Module-topology___-noetherian-rings-and-the-structure-sheaf":{"contents":"For finitely generated modules carrying the canonical module topology over a complete\nTate ring, every surjective -linear map is open\n(), and short exact sequences are\nstrict () (). This is the open-mapping\ntheorem for module topologies. Following Wedhorn Proposition 6.18.\n\nA finitely generated module is an open quotient of some  with the product\ntopology; a surjection  lifts to a surjection\n, and completeness plus finite generation make this quotient\nmap open (a Banach-style open mapping argument). Openness of the surjection and\ncontinuity of the injection in a short exact sequence is exactly strictness. Following\nWedhorn Proposition 6.18.\n\nIn a complete Tate ring that is noetherian, every ideal is closed\n(, ). In Adic-Spaces:\n. Following Wedhorn Proposition 6.17.\n\nAn ideal  is a finitely generated submodule, hence (by the open-mapping theorem for\nthe module topology, ) carries the subspace topology as an\nopen quotient of . The quotient  with the module topology is complete and\nHausdorff, which forces  to be closed. Following Wedhorn\nProposition 6.17.\n\nThe adic spectrum packaged as a topological space (object of ),\n, together with its continuous support map to \n() (, ). This is the\nunderlying space on which the structure (pre)sheaf lives. Following Wedhorn §8.1.\n\nThe type-level part of the structure-sheaf condition holds unconditionally: the\nunderlying  of the \"locally-a-fraction\" predicate on\n is a sheaf of types\n(, ). In Adic-Spaces:\n — this is the sorry-free core extracted\nfrom the (still in-progress) topological-ring sheaf statement. Following Wedhorn §8.1\n(Theorem 8.28(c)).\n\nSections of the structure presheaf are by definition functions to stalks that are\nlocally given by a fraction; this is a local predicate, and the subpresheaf of all\nfunctions satisfying a local predicate is automatically a sheaf of types (gluing is\npointwise on stalks and the local condition is checked on the cover). The remaining,\ngenuinely in-progress, content is upgrading this from  to topological\nrings (Tate acyclicity), which still carries s. Following Wedhorn §8.1.\n\n(Tate's acyclicity theorem / the structure sheaf — in progress.) For a strongly\nnoetherian (stably uniform) Tate ring, the structure presheaf  on the\nrational subsets (, ,\n) of  is conjecturally a sheaf of\ntopological rings: the Čech complex of a rational (Laurent) cover should be exact (Tate\nacyclicity), so sections glue, making  an\nadic space. The type-level sheaf condition is already established\n(); in Adic-Spaces the ring-valued\n (Adic spaces/StructureSheaf.lean) and the\nLaurent-cover acyclicity carry the remaining s. Following Wedhorn\nTheorem 8.28(b,c).\n\n","context":"Adic Spaces — Lean blueprint","header":"10. Module topology, noetherian rings and the structure sheaf","id":"/Module-topology___-noetherian-rings-and-the-structure-sheaf/#Adic-Spaces-___-Lean-blueprint--Module-topology___-noetherian-rings-and-the-structure-sheaf"}});